  "script": "Every purchase needed a printed approval sheet. Our new manager said digital approvals were too easy to miss, so before ordering anything we had to print the request, the email thread, and every attachment. Fine. We followed that rule with the devotion it clearly deserved. A replacement keyboard produced fourteen pages. A software renewal arrived with a seventy-page license. By Wednesday, stacks of approval paper covered the supply counter, and Facilities delivered three more boxes of paper just to approve buying two boxes of paper. That little circle of life was apparently billable. Then the toner bill reached Finance. They compared it with our actual purchases and found the paperwork cost more than the supplies it approved. The manager called an efficiency meeting, pulled down the rule, and restored the digital form. The recycling bins got the final approval: no attachment required.",
